WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.337
CMMC assessments provide a structured way to verify whether required cybersecurity practices

00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:08.393
are implemented within a defined assessment scope.

00:00:08.393 --> 00:00:14.503
The required assessment type depends on the CMMC level and the applicable acquisition requirement.

00:00:14.503 --> 00:00:17.558
A self-assessment is still a formal representation.

00:00:17.558 --> 00:00:21.486
The organization needs knowledgeable personnel, accurate scope, reliable evidence,

00:00:21.486 --> 00:00:22.359
documented results,

00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:27.596
and accountable affirmation rather than an informal opinion about general security maturity.

00:00:27.596 --> 00:00:33.707
Some Level 2 contracts require an assessment by an authorized CMMC Third-Party Assessment Organization.

00:00:33.707 --> 00:00:38.508
Level 3 uses the government assessment role established by the program.

00:00:38.508 --> 00:00:43.309
Organizations should confirm current assessor and status requirements through official channels.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:45.927
Scope is a critical starting point.

00:00:45.927 --> 00:00:50.728
The assessment needs a clear understanding of systems, people, facilities, services,

00:00:50.728 --> 00:00:52.474
security-protection assets, external providers,

00:00:52.474 --> 00:00:56.402
and information flows associated with the covered contract information.

00:00:56.402 --> 00:01:00.767
Evidence may include policies, procedures, configurations, logs, tickets, training records,

00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:04.258
diagrams, inventories, approvals, technical outputs, interviews, and demonstrations.

00:01:04.258 --> 00:01:09.932
Strong evidence reflects normal operation rather than material created only for an assessment.

00:01:09.932 --> 00:01:13.424
Assessment results can identify findings that require action.

00:01:13.424 --> 00:01:18.225
Program rules govern status, remediation, any permitted plan of action, affirmation,

00:01:18.225 --> 00:01:23.899
and reporting, so organizations should not assume that every gap can be deferred.

00:01:23.899 --> 00:01:27.827
Verification is a point-in-time activity supported by ongoing responsibility.

00:01:27.827 --> 00:01:31.318
Systems change, employees move, suppliers change, configurations drift,

00:01:31.318 --> 00:01:35.246
and new vulnerabilities emerge after an assessment has ended.

00:01:35.246 --> 00:01:38.302
Audit readiness is therefore an operational condition.

00:01:38.302 --> 00:01:41.793
Clear ownership, current documentation, repeatable controls, retained evidence,

00:01:41.793 --> 00:01:42.666
internal review,

00:01:42.666 --> 00:01:48.340
and timely remediation make assessment results more accurate and security outcomes more sustainable.
